[Event "King's Gambit Accepted"]

{2.f4 offers a pawn to deflect e5 and build a centre with d4, opening the f-file. Sharp and romantic, but playable at club level.}
1. e4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
1... e5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8 and the bishop on f8.}
2. f4 {[%cal Gf4e5] The King's Gambit: White offers a pawn to remove e5.}
2... exf4 {Accepts. Takes the pawn on f4, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
3. Nf3 {Develops and stops ...Qh4+. The knight on f3 controls e5 and d4.}
3... g5 {Black tries to hold f4. Gains space on the kingside; the pawn on g5 takes f4 and h4 away from the opponent.}
 (3... Nf6 {The Schallopp: develops and hits e4. The knight on f6 controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
4. e5 {[%cal Ge5f6] Gains space with tempo. Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d6 and eyes the knight on f6.}
4... Nh5 {The knight guards f4. Moves the knight out of attack to h5.}
5. d4 {[%csl Gd4,Ge5] Strong centre; the h5-knight is offside. The pawn on d4 controls c5 and supports the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
)
 (3... d5 {The modern antidote: return the pawn for free development.}
4. exd5 {Takes the pawn on d5; Black will want to recapture.}
4... Nf6 {Develops and eyes d5. The knight on f6 controls e4 and eyes the pawn on d5. That pawn is currently undefended.}
5. Nc3 {Develops and defends d5. The knight on c3 controls e4 and supports the pawn on d5.}
5... Nxd5 {Regains the pawn. Takes the pawn on d5; White will want to recapture.}
6. Nxd5 {Recaptures on d5, restoring the material balance.}
6... Qxd5 {Recaptures on d5, restoring the material balance.}
7. d4 {[%csl Gd4] Takes the centre and opens the c1-bishop toward f4.}
)
4. h4 {[%cal Gh4g5] Undermines the g5 chain at once. Gains space on the kingside; the pawn on h4 takes g5 away from the opponent. Black's last move put a pawn on g5, which this now pressures.}
4... g4 {Gains time on the knight. The pawn on g4 controls h3 and eyes the knight on f3.}
5. Ne5 {The Kieseritzky Gambit: the knight goes forward to a central square.}
5... Nf6 {Develops and hits e4. The knight on f6 controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
6. d4 {[%csl Ge5,Gd4] Supports the e5 knight. Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and supports the knight on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
6... d6 {Kicks the knight. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and eyes the knight on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
7. Nd3 {Retreats where it attacks f4. The knight goes to d3 and controls e5. It attacks the pawn on f4, which is undefended.}
7... Nxe4 {Takes e4. Takes the pawn on e4, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
8. Bxf4 {[%csl Gf4] White regains a pawn with open lines and active pieces.} *

[Event "King's Gambit Declined & Falkbeer"]

{Not everyone accepts. Know the Classical Declined (2...Bc5) and the Falkbeer (2...d5).}
1. e4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
1... e5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8 and the bishop on f8.}
2. f4 {The King's Gambit. Supports the centre: the pawn on f4 controls g5 and eyes the pawn on e5. That pawn is currently undefended.}
2... Bc5 {[%cal Gc5g1] Declines and stops White castling short. Develops the bishop to c5, where it controls d4.}
 (2... d5 {The Falkbeer Countergambit. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c4 and eyes the pawn on e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
3. exd5 {Takes the pawn on d5; Black will want to recapture.}
3... exf4 {Black transposes into an accepted line with ...d5.}
4. Nf3 {Develops and covers h4. The knight on f3 controls e5 and d4.}
4... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and eyes the pawn on d5. That pawn is currently undefended.}
5. Nc3 {Develops and holds d5. The knight on c3 controls e4 and supports the pawn on d5.}
5... Nxd5 {Regains the pawn. Takes the pawn on d5; White will want to recapture.}
6. Nxd5 {Recaptures on d5, restoring the material balance.}
6... Qxd5 {Recaptures on d5, restoring the material balance.}
7. d4 {Centre and the bishop goes to f4. The pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
)
3. Nf3 {Develops and attacks e5. The knight on f3 controls d4 and eyes the pawn on e5. That pawn is currently undefended.}
3... d6 {Supports e5. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 supports the bishop on c5 and the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
4. c3 {[%csl Gd4] Prepares d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c3 controls b4 and d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
4... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5. d4 {[%cal Gd4c5] Builds the centre with tempo on the bishop. The pawn on d4 eyes the bishop on c5 and the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
5... exd4 {Takes the pawn on d4; White will want to recapture.}
6. cxd4 {Recaptures on d4, restoring the material balance.}
6... Bb4+ {Check. It is check, so the reply is forced. Moves the bishop out of attack to b4. It gives check.}
7. Bd2 {Blocks and offers a trade. Develops the bishop to d2, where it controls e3 and supports the pawn on f4. It attacks the bishop on b4, which is undefended.} *
